title: LSP Editor Setup

!! Connecting an editor

!!! Editors that attach to a socket

Neovim connects to a server that is already running, which is the mode that shares a process with `--mcp`. Register the filetype first, since `.tid` is not one Neovim knows:

```
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { tid = "tid" } })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("FileType", {
	pattern = "tid",
	callback = function()
		vim.lsp.start({
			name = "tiddlywiki",
			cmd = vim.lsp.rpc.connect("127.0.0.1", 6009),
			root_dir = vim.fn.getcwd(),
		})
	end,
})
```

!!! Editors that launch a command

Helix and most others expect to start the server themselves and talk to it over stdin and stdout. That needs its own TiddlyWiki process, because `--mcp` cannot share stdio (see [[LSP and MCP]]):

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --lsp stdio
```

!!! VS Code

The [[vscode-tiddlywiki-lsp|https://github.com/wikilabs/vscode-tiddlywiki-lsp]] extension starts the wiki's LSP server itself and ends it with the window; [[LSP Configuration]] says how it picks the wiki, and its README how to install it. Without a configured wiki it connects to one already running with `--lsp`. It binds to the `tid` and `tiddlywiki5` languages the tw5-syntax extension registers, and the status bar names the LSP server the window uses.

While the linked MCP server serves a browser, right-click a `.tid` tab and pick //Preview in TiddlyWiki//: the running wiki opens beside the editor on that tiddler, and a save shows up there without a refresh.

A shadow tiddler, or a tiddler without a file of its own, opens read-only under the extension's `tiddlywiki:` scheme when you go to a definition in it or pick it from a list. After updating the extension, reload the VS Code window so the scheme is registered; when its `package.json` changed, restart VS Code.

!! Saving

The server reads a `.tid` you save in the editor into its wiki: hovers, globals and references then answer from the saved text, with no `reload_tiddlers` and no restart. Only a file the wiki already files a tiddler in, or a new `.tid` in its tiddlers folder, is read, so a plugin's source file never becomes a tiddler of its own. When a save changes a tiddler's title, the new title takes the file and the old one leaves the wiki. The server log names each tiddler it reloads.

An LSP server the editor started holds its own copy of the wiki, so it passes the save on to a running MCP server as well, and the browser and MCP tools see it too. See [[LSP and MCP]].

The editor has to tell the server about saves; VS Code does. Try it in [[LSP Saving - (Example)]].
